INT. ROLLER RINK - AFTER CLOSING - NIGHT

Half the lights are off. The disco ball turns slowly over an empty floor. Rental skates hang in pairs along a wall.

JUNE ABARA, 16, sits on the carpeted bench lacing a skate she has already laced. Her brother CALVIN, 19, stacks chairs with his back to her.

RAFA, 17, and PRIYA, 16, hover near the snack counter, sharing one bag of chips and not eating them.

MR. DUBOIS, 55, rink manager, jingles keys by the front door.

MR. DUBOIS
Ten minutes, kids. I'm not paying the electric for a goodbye.

Nobody moves. Calvin sets one chair down harder than needed.

RAFA
You could have said something, June.

JUNE
I said it.

RAFA
Not to her.

Priya looks at the floor. June pulls the lace tighter and the skate goes white at the toe.

CALVIN
(not turning)
Mom's in the car.

JUNE
I know.

CALVIN
She's been in the car forty minutes.

June stands. She wobbles, steadies, rolls two feet toward the floor, and stops at the edge like it is a curb.

PRIYA
I'll go with you.

RAFA
I'll go with you.

They say it together. Both stop. Neither looks at the other.

PRIYA
I'll go with you.
^RAFA
I'll go with you.

JUNE
(a small laugh that dies)
You two are the worst.

She rolls out onto the floor. One slow lap. Priya and Rafa watch. Calvin has stopped stacking.

Halfway round she tips her head back to the disco ball. It throws small white dots across her face, and she lets them.

MR. DUBOIS
June. That's time.

She finishes the lap. At the bench she sits, unlaces the skate, and hands it to Calvin. He takes it without a word and puts it on the shelf. He holds her shoe out. She puts it on.

At the door, Priya hands her the bag of chips. June takes one. Eats it. Gives the bag back.

EXT. ROLLER RINK PARKING LOT - CONTINUOUS

A beat-up minivan idles, dome light on. Inside, a woman's shape, hands on the wheel. June walks to the passenger door and gets in. She does not close it right away.
